Draw engine: keep a supersampled resolve on the GPU.

Soulcalibur II HD renders its scene into a 2x supersampled surface, resolves it with six 512x512 NV3089 2:1 tiles
into VRAM (0x0C900000, 1280x720) and draws that to the display buffer with one full-screen pass. Every frame the
engine read the whole scene back (a flush and wait mid-frame), stored it into guest memory, and the full-screen pass
then re-hashed, decoded and uploaded the same 3.6 MB as a texture.

- A resolve tile is now copied on the GPU (new optional backend call color_target_copy; Vulkan: vkCmdCopyImage in
  the batch) into a colour target the engine keeps for that guest image, with the rectangles that are newer there
  than in guest memory. A texture unit reading exactly that image (linear A8R8G8B8, its address, pitch and size,
  one level) with all of it resolved samples the target through the unit's crossbar.
- Guest memory gets the pixels when anything else reads or writes those bytes through the engine: a texture of
  another shape, a 2D transfer's source or destination (rsx_draw_engine_guest_access, called by NV3089), a surface
  seed or a depth store. A CPU read of the resolved bytes is not seen; RSX_ENGINE_GPU_RESOLVE=0 restores the store of
  every resolve. Checked on the same frames against the old path: identical output.

